- How would label delay or feedback latency affect retraining cadence?
- How would you monitor for false positives increasing over time and adapt thresholds?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical, CPU-friendly baseline skills for streaming ML with mixed data types (tabular and text) and delayed labels. It evaluates understanding of online learning, feature engineering for text, calibration, and drift handling in production systems.

## Key Concepts

- Multimodal features (numeric telemetry + text diagnostic logs)
- Online learning via partial_fit for streaming updates
- Feature hashing (hashing trick) for text data
- Drift detection using KL divergence
- Probability calibration for reliable confidence scores
- Ensemble methods for combining model streams

## Code Example

```javascript
class BaselineModel {
  constructor() {
    this.scaler = new StandardScaler();
    this.numericModel = new LogisticRegression();
    this.textModel = new LogisticRegression();
  }

  partialFitNumeric(X, y) {
    const Xscaled = this.scaler.partialFit(X);
    this.numericModel.partialFit(Xscaled, y);
  }

  partialFitText(textData, y) {
    const Xhashed = this.hashText(textData);
    this.textModel.partialFit(Xhashed, y);
  }

  predict(probaNumeric, probaText) {
    return (probaNumeric + probaText) / 2;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical, beginner-friendly ML pipeline for churn with real-world constraints: leakage prevention, encoding choices, and model comparisons that balance performance and interpretability.

## Key Concepts

- Data leakage prevention via time-based or grouped splits
- Handling class imbalance in binary targets
- Baseline vs tree-based models and interpretability
- Evaluation metrics: AUROC andPR-AUC, plus feature importance tools like SHAP

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo baseline pipeline (illustrative only)
function trainBaseline(data){
  const X = oneHotEncode(data.features);
  const {train, test} = timeSplit(X, data.labels);
  const model = trainLR(train.features, train.labels, {C:1.0});
  const preds = model.predict(test.features);
  return evaluate(test.labels, preds);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a much larger dataset?
- What monitoring would you set up to detect data drift over time?

Why This Is Asked
